  "query_preview": "..issued 0304am. \nCurrent regional analysis shows an upper level low moving\nonshore over northern Baja and SoCal. A few lightning strikes\nare evident with some of the stronger convection moving \nonshore. This will traverse our area later today, brining \nscattered to numerous showers (30-80%) and isolated \nthunderstorms (10-30%). While overall areal averages of rainfall\nwill be anywhere from a 0.1\" to 0.35\", localized amounts under \nheavier showers or storms could see upwards of 0.6\"-0.9\". \nRRFS/REFS and HRRR show an organized line of showers with \nembedded storms to the east and northeast of the upper level low\ncenter. With 25-40 kts of 0-6km shear and 200-700 j/kg of \nSBCAPE, we cannot rule out a strong storm or two with small \nhail, heavy rain, and gusty winds to 45 mph. The probs of gust\nin excess of 45 mph are 10-20% with pockets of 40%. REFS also\nshow some isolated and low probs of a gust or two to 58mph. \nRRFS also shows this potential as the line of storms become more\norganized as it pushes across the Tucson Metro. The best chance\nfor any isolated severe gusts would be NE of Tucson where the \ngreatest probs exist. While we are mentioning this, the overall\nprob of happening are quite low.  \n\nIf some of the CAM solutions are correct, there could be some\nminor/nuisance flooding across the metro depending on rainfall\nrates. Not expecting any flash flooding or anything signfciant.\n\nThe system departs fairly quickly by this evening with \nlingering showers into the late evening. Enough moisture and \ninstability will be around for Game Day Festivities on Saturday,\nbut should only amount to sprinkles or a very brief light \nshower. Temps look pretty great with highs around 70 degrees \nacross the Metro. Bear Down.  \n\nOnce this system passes, there will be shortwave high pressure \nallowing for a dry period Sunday and Monday. Daytime highs will\nbe in the 70s lower deserts and 60s higher desert valleys. \n\nThe second system begins to dig down the West Coast and brings \nthe next round of precipitation late overnight Monday into late\novernight Tuesday, with some lingering showers into Wednesday \nmorning. The models continue to have different timing as to when\nthis system weakens as a third system digs down behind it late \nnext week. Regardless, rainfall totals of 0.01\"-0.60\" is \npossible with the highest amounts in the mountains, and in \nPinal County and along the International Border. Snow levels may\ncome down low enough for some flurries at the top of Mt Graham \nand White Mountains, but no accumulation is expected. \nTemperatures begin to decrease to below normal, with the coolest\nday on Wednesday.\n\nThis active pattern looks to continue into next weekend with a\nthird system, with more details being ironed out as we get\ncloser and models come into more agreement.",
